<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Humphrey Gilbert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1539–<a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sir Humphrey Gilbert, Gilbert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Humphrey Gilbert was an English soldier and explorer of the Elizabethan era. In 1583 he made a formal claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> for the English crown, but was lost at sea on the return voyage and left no settlers. His claim is conventionally treated as the founding moment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert.jpg" alt="Humphrey Gilbert" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Humphrey_Gilbert_Compton_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founding moment is conventionally placed at <b>Sir Humphrey Gilbert</b>'s formal claim to Newfoundland in 1583, though Gilbert was lost at sea on the return voyage and no settlers were left behind.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…a set of variations on the song in 1959, on An Evening Wasted with Tom Lehrer, to show his theory that &quot;folk songs are so atrocious because they were written by the people&quot;, singing its verses in the styles of Cole Porter, Mozart, bebop and <b>Gilbert</b> and Sullivan.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a></li><li><blockquote>Its current form was first published in Carols Ancient and Modern (1823) and in <b>Gilbert</b> and Sandys Carols (1833), collections edited by William Sandys and arranged and edited by Davies Gilbert, who also wrote extra words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-first-nowell">The First Nowell</a></li><li><blockquote>The song is absent from the carol collections of the West-countrymen Davies <b>Gilbert</b> (1822 and 1823) and William Sandys (1833), from the anthologies of Sylvester (1861) and Husk (1864), and from The Oxford Book of Carols (1928).</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-wish-you-a-merry-christmas">We Wish You a Merry Christmas</a></li><li><blockquote>At St John's harbour, <b>Humphrey Gilbert</b> claimed Newfoundland for Elizabeth I, an early step in English overseas expansion.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gilbert's</b> son Richard de Clare, Strongbow, led the invasion of Ireland in 1170 from here.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-pembroke">County Palatine of Pembroke</a></li></ul></section>
